The commands that turn a booted machine into an NVKE node, run before kubeadm.
Rendered by BuildForgeNodePayloads, one command per non-blank line, and sent as
NodePoolCreateConfig.infra.preKubeadmCommands.

The package versions are inputs for the same reason the OS image is: a
qualification run exists to pin them.

The apt suite is one too. NVKE publishes a suite per Ubuntu release, and a node
booted from a 26.04 image cannot install from the 24.04 one, so hardcoding
either suite pins which OS images the lane can qualify. See
docs/site-bootstrap/pre-provisioned-nodes.md and
agent/scripts/upload-s3-apt-intake.sh, which publishes both.

Both OVN packages, not just -images. The agent resolves the charts package
name and requires it to already be installed: chartsDebPresenceCheck returns
errCNIChartsDebNotInstalled and the agent never installs it itself. That
failure terminates the provisioning workflow rather than retrying, so putting
the package on afterwards does not recover the run. openvswitch-switch is here
for the same reason — OVN runs Open vSwitch on the host, and the agent checks
for ovs-vsctl before helm. Both were observed on real Forge hardware before
they were read out of the agent source.

nke-agent is deliberately absent, for the reason spire-agent is. The image
builds it as a .deb from this checkout's agent/ source at bake time and
installs it with dpkg, so its version is the checkout's `git describe` and it
is the build under qualification (os-image/AGENTS.md). The apt repository
publishes its own nke-agent, and installing that unpinned would swap the
component being qualified for whichever one the suite currently holds, without
the run saying so. Today dpkg keeps the baked build because a bake version
sorts above a release tag, so the install was a no-op that would have stopped
being one at the next publish.

spire-agent is deliberately absent too. The NVKE OS image pre-installs it, from a
.deb built at bake time by os-image/packages/spire-agent/ which also owns the
systemd units, the first-boot renderer and the TPM plugin checksum that
agent.conf resolves against (os-image/SPEC.md). Installing the apt repository's
build over it swaps a package the image owns for a generic one, and because
the repository publishes 1.14.1+tpm1.9.0 against the image's
1.14.1+tpm1.9.0-1, doing so is a downgrade to an identical build of itself.
A node without a baked agent is the bring-your-own-Ubuntu path, which
bootstraps with --disable-mtls and is out of scope here.

The apt source is fetched by name, so the node must be able to resolve one
before the curl below. A default IPv4 route is not that: the route guard in
RenderUserData returns as soon as one appears, and systemd-resolved can still
be coming up -- on a node whose clock is at 1970, NTP has not run either. The
curl then fails with "Could not resolve host", set -e kills the block, and the
node ends up with no kubeadm, no CNI and no agent args, failing much later with
nothing pointing back here. Observed on three consecutive az61 runs and
reproduced by hand. Waiting for the name costs nothing when it already
resolves.

The apt coordinates are nvke-, and the packages are split. Both halves are
measured against the live repository, not inferred, because the rename landed
in three pieces that completed at different times.

nvke-apt-public.key and nvke-apt-<codename> serving suite nvke-<codename> are
published and answer 200. nke/packages/apt-publisher now mirrors into both, so
the nvke suite is a superset: it carries the renamed packages AND the ones
still spelled the old way. Pointing at it is therefore strictly better than the
old repo, which has only the old names.

nvke-kubeadm-distro-<ver> is published there -- 1.36.3 confirmed in both noble
and resolute on 2026-10-14 -- so it moved with the coordinates.

nvke-cni-ovn-images and nvke-cni-ovn-charts are published by #1560. Until that
lands the suite serves only nke-cni-ovn-*, and asking for a name it does not
serve is a "no candidate" that terminates ProvisionCluster through
InstallCNIPreconditionFailed, which does not retry.

The unit and its environment file stay nke-, permanently. nvke-agent.service
declares Alias=nke-agent.service and reads both environment files, so the old
spelling works on images built before and after #1565 and the new one only
after. Do not "finish" this rename.

set -eu is the first command for the reason the site node has it
(internal/infra/provision/userdata.go): cloud-init runs these as one /bin/sh
script and reports the exit status of the last one, which is the
touch at the end. Without the guard a failed apt step still writes the
success sentinel, so the node reports a clean cloud-init while missing the
packages it needs to join, never phones home, and the run spends its whole
budget on KubernetesReady=Unknown ("Node data not available") with nothing
saying why. set -u is safe because every command below is fully rendered
before it is sent: none of them reads a shell variable.

The route wait comes straight after it, before the first command that needs
the network. Forge hosts race cloud-init for the network, so on a node whose
default route is not up yet the curl below fails at once, set -eu stops the
script there, and cloud-init reports status: error with nothing installed.
The amd64 kernel line in nico_forge_ipxe.tmpl does not carry ip=dhcp the way
the arm64 one does, so amd64 nodes reach this point earlier relative to their
network. Run 37873467928 is that case: an amd64 control plane failed
cloud-init while an arm64 worker running these same commands passed, and the
amd64 site node beside them passed only because its own user data waits for
a route first. Bounded, so a node that never gets a route fails here with a
line saying so rather than hanging the boot.
*/ -}}
set -eu
timeout 300s sh -c 'until ip -4 route list default | grep -q .; do echo "waiting for an IPv4 default route"; sleep 5; done'
install -d -m 0755 /etc/apt/keyrings
timeout 300s sh -c 'until getent hosts nke-prod-assets.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com >/dev/null 2>&1; do echo "waiting for name resolution"; sleep 5; done'
curl -fsSL -o /etc/apt/keyrings/nvke.asc https://nke-prod-assets.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/nvke-apt-public.key
echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/nvke.asc] https://nke-prod-assets.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/nvke-apt-{{ .AptSuite }} nvke-{{ .AptSuite }} main' > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/nvke-apt.list
apt-get update
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y nvke-kubeadm-distro-{{ .KubernetesVersion }}
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y nvke-cni-ovn-images-{{ .CNIVersion }} nvke-cni-ovn-charts-{{ .CNIVersion }}
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y openvswitch-switch
printf 'AGENT_ARGS="{{ .AgentArgs }}"\n' > /etc/default/nke-agent
sysctl --system
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart nke-agent.service
systemctl restart procps
touch /run/cloud-init.done
